-- Shorter public introductions for Calder's Run residents.
-- Exact-match guards preserve later edits and leave custom characters alone.
with edits(id, old_bio, new_bio) as (
  values
  ('8d8a8b9e-aac5-5c86-883c-e55d5c1245ba'::uuid, 'Adelaide inherited management during a period when her household’s reputation exceeded its available cash. She restored productivity, negotiated credit, and learned to treat a promise as something that must be funded. Her ranch became a place where guests could enjoy generous meals without seeing the labor and anxiety supporting them. Widowhood left her free to make decisions and accustomed to not explaining them. She loves Hazel intensely and struggles to recognize how often support becomes supervision. Her defense of local independence is sincere, but she has benefited from arrangements that deny smaller neighbors the same freedom.', 'Adelaide runs Whitcomb Ranch and rebuilt it when the family had more reputation than cash. She''s generous at dinner and tough in business. She loves Hazel, though she often tries to manage her daughter''s choices.'),
  ('2a838e0f-f2e8-582a-8925-1380e8f9cc3b'::uuid, 'Agnes built the boardinghouse after widowhood forced her to turn years of household competence into a public business. She knows the difference between a boarder who needs a little time and an employer using her patience to subsidize late wages. Her rooms have become a reliable starting point for people trying to establish themselves in town. She is warm, observant, and capable of a very final refusal. What she misses most is being invited somewhere as a guest. She wants a companion who will notice when she has finished serving and make room for her at the table.', 'Agnes owns the Boatmen''s Boardinghouse and knows which late payer needs patience and which employer is passing the bill to her. She''s warm with her boarders but wishes someone would invite her to dinner for a change.'),
  ('78bb861c-ec4e-5acb-9a14-211d669a33c6'::uuid, 'Alma came west with relatives working in kitchens and freight hospitality, then chose Calder’s Run because its late workers needed better food than they were getting. At twenty-one she has considerable kitchen experience and limited authority over the business that profits from it. She is inventive without treating every meal as a performance. Feeding tired people well gives her satisfaction; being expected to mother them all does not. A proposed partnership could make her secure if its obligations and power were balanced. She likes music from another room, dancing when her feet permit, and friends who can laugh at an unsuccessful experiment.', 'Alma cooks for Calder''s Run''s late workers and makes better food than her bosses expect. She''s thinking about a partnership, but only if she gets a real say in the kitchen she helps keep open.'),
  ('de185ba5-cd6b-5022-80cd-8669cc8cfaf2'::uuid, 'August is eighteen and works as cemetery keeper and an undertaker’s assistant under established local supervision. He chose the apprenticeship for steady work and discovered that practical care could make a terrible day slightly easier for a family. He is not fascinated by death; he enjoys books, music, gardening, and jokes with Nell that other people seldom hear. Residents sometimes speak to him as if the job made him either much older or incapable of ordinary pleasure. He wants competence, friendship, and a future in which his trade is one part of his identity rather than the answer to every question about him.', 'August keeps the cemetery and assists the undertaker. He''s good with grieving families, but he''d rather be asked about his books, garden, or the joke Nell told him yesterday.'),
  ('2005abeb-d903-5a5a-a1cb-296fdd68b02a'::uuid, 'Beatrice learned bakery work from relatives and then chose to pursue it as a trade rather than treat it as an inevitable domestic duty. At twenty she knows how to organize a demanding morning and how easily a large order can consume the profit it appears to promise. She is reserved with strangers but not uncertain about her values. Friendship with Daisy and affection for her uncle Jonah give her a community that sometimes assumes she will always be available. She enjoys detailed craft, quiet jokes, and the rare satisfaction of spending an afternoon doing nothing productive.', 'Beatrice bakes at Hearthstone and knows a big order can lose money as easily as make it. She''s reserved with strangers, close to Daisy and her uncle Jonah, and protective of her rare afternoons off.'),
  ('b884dee9-b258-5d00-97cd-16a401685fa6'::uuid, 'Ben is twenty-two and works as a checkweigher after entering mine employment as an adult. The 1881 collapse marked his household when he was younger and left him alert to the difference between a company account and what reaches a family. He has become a crew representative because he listens, keeps records, and does not promise a victory merely to sound strong. He likes music, substantial meals, and the relief of being able to laugh without feeling he has forgotten someone’s suffering. A wage discrepancy is becoming too consistent to dismiss, and he fears the cost of both speaking and remaining quiet.', 'Ben checks mine weights and keeps records for his crew. He remembers what the old collapse cost his family, and a new wage discrepancy is making it hard to stay quiet.'),
  ('48948de7-27bd-5a3c-8053-3afa6e59f55d'::uuid, 'Bess is twenty-one and works at the Red Sash as a sex worker and evening entertainer. She arrived through ordinary lodging and performance work, compared offers, and chose a house where she could set appointments and keep her own money. She enjoys the company, the clothes, and being good at reading a room; she dislikes the assumption that enjoying the work means having no complaints about it. She wants residents to have a formal voice in house decisions and dreams of producing a comic evening with Cora and Rosalie. Sabine’s lease plan could give that ambition a foundation, or tie their savings to a building before the terms are sound.', 'Bess performs and works at the Red Sash, where she sets her own appointments and keeps her earnings. She wants the women who live there to have a say in house decisions and hopes to put on a comedy night.'),
  ('19b5e0c0-f297-5fe5-a3c9-cc929e310dd3'::uuid, 'Caleb learned river craft around his father’s ferry and chose boatbuilding because it could turn inherited knowledge into something visibly his own. At twenty he is skilled at ordinary repair and still learning how to cost an ambitious commission. The bridge looks to him like a reason to invent a new service; he thinks Samuel sees only loss. In fact, both have privately imagined similar futures. Caleb is sociable, affectionate, and impatient with arguments he thinks are about pride. He does not always recognize his own pride in wanting to be the person who first saw the answer.', 'Caleb builds and repairs boats on his father''s river. He sees the new bridge as a chance to try something different, though he and Samuel argue before either hears the other''s idea.'),
  ('227c6912-aa1c-56e7-ac17-06e97a40ffab'::uuid, 'Clara grew up watching Daniel turn technical precision into a reliable livelihood and wanted a craft whose result people could hold. She learned photography through patient assisting, study, and a succession of disappointing prints. At twenty she has opened a small studio with her own savings and a clearly recorded family loan. She enjoys the moment when a sitter stops performing the face they think a photograph requires. Her commercial work pays the bills; her private ambition is a series of ordinary town portraits that show workers as people with lives beyond their occupations.', 'Clara opened a photography studio with her savings and a loan from her family. Paid portraits keep it running; the pictures she really wants are of workers when they''re off the clock.'),
  ('bac209b8-8ee8-50c9-894d-5b767392884a'::uuid, 'Cole is twenty-two, a former railway laborer whose grievance against a company became useful to a man with fewer scruples about what followed. He joined Silas after a disputed dismissal and participated in the theft of a consignment he was told belonged to an investor. Discovering that part of it was ordinary workers’ pay broke the story he had been telling himself. He remains responsible for choosing the work and frightening people during it. Around camp he repairs everyday equipment, reads borrowed adventure stories, and makes jokes a moment before admitting he is afraid. Leaving would require money, testimony, and an answer to people who have good reason not to trust him.', 'Cole joined a bandit crew after losing his railroad job. He thought their latest theft hit an investor, then learned they had taken workers'' pay. He wants out, but the people he frightened have no reason to trust him yet.'),
  ('c08888b0-bc02-502f-a0ac-eacfef8b5388'::uuid, 'Cora began earning money as a musician’s assistant and learned to call dances by listening to what confused people rather than copying a grand style. She is nineteen, already good at a specialized job, and tired of employers describing a successful evening as fortunate. Calder’s Run gives her a recognizable place in public life and a circle of friends who expect her to make every gathering enjoyable. She likes that role until she needs someone else to make the effort. Her ambition is a fair share of the hall’s receipts and the freedom to perform beyond the town without being treated as disloyal.', 'Cora calls dances in Calder''s Run and knows how to keep a crowded hall moving. She wants a fair share of the receipts and a chance to perform elsewhere without being called disloyal.'),
  ('96098866-3090-5564-9617-ac35b34cbd35'::uuid, 'Daisy is eighteen and apprenticed in a dairy cooperative where a working day begins before the people praising fresh cream have risen. She is cheerful when rested and considerably less saintly at the end of an extra shift. She keeps careful experiments because she wants her contribution to be identifiable instead of absorbed into general praise for the business. Friends know her as an eager reader of letters from elsewhere and a surprisingly sharp judge of an implausible boast. She wants to travel someday, but first she would like to make something that people ask for by name.', 'Daisy works at the dairy cooperative and gets tired of hearing how easy fresh cream must be. She keeps notes on her experiments because she wants people to ask for something she made by name.'),
  ('39ed7112-d367-5790-a841-f76c9eab8052'::uuid, 'Daniel learned telegraph work through training and careful service that made employers trust him with information they would not discuss in public. He is thirty and established in the railhead office, where he tries to keep a useful institution from becoming an informal listening post. His younger sister Clara has opened a studio with a family loan they recorded because both want affection to survive business disagreement. He likes photography himself, dry comedy, and conversations that wander once work is over. He is proud of being dependable and sometimes hides how much pressure that reputation places on him.', 'Daniel runs the telegraph desk at the railhead and takes care with messages people would rather keep private. He''s proud of his sister Clara''s studio, loves a dry joke, and could use an evening when nobody needs him.'),
  ('8394a4a8-7d5f-5f8f-bbbf-0a21e60b8c90'::uuid, 'Lucía trained through formal study and demanding supervised practice before choosing a town where a physician could build continuity with families instead of treating a procession of strangers. She expanded the clinic with paid work, donations, and an exhausting number of calls. Her niece Inés now lives nearby and resists being managed as another patient. Lucía is excellent at recognizing the practical need in front of her and less good at noticing when work has become a way to avoid decisions about her own life. She enjoys cards, gardens, and argumentative supper company when someone persuades her to stop writing lists.', 'Lucía runs Calder''s Run''s clinic and knows most families by name. She''s great at seeing what a patient needs, less good at leaving her own work behind for supper or a game of cards.'),
  ('2aedb376-b459-54f7-ae2e-4a2251f295c7'::uuid, 'Edith migrated as a child with a household accustomed to moving where work could be found. She learned laundry and maintenance accounting before joining the baths, where her clear records prevented arguments staff used to lose by default. She is twenty-two and proud of making an unglamorous business reliable. Outside work she likes fine clothes, architecture, and the pleasure of being looked at when she has chosen it. People mistake her measured manner for compliance. She is capable of leaving a comfortable arrangement abruptly once she concludes that negotiation is being used only to delay her.', 'Edith keeps the baths'' books in order and knows exactly what the staff are owed. People mistake her calm voice for agreement. She likes fine clothes and buildings with good lines.'),
  ('fa092c09-c4c0-5ca4-8482-1c00cc7e5897'::uuid, 'Eliza arrived after training and supervised teaching with a small collection of books and a conviction that adult embarrassment should not be part of learning. At twenty-one she manages a modest classroom and runs occasional adult sessions whose students include workers older than she is. Her pupils’ daytime lives remain background activity; her companion story concerns her own adult friendships and ambitions. She wants further education and perhaps travel, yet the town has begun to make staying feel meaningful. She can be quietly stubborn, especially when someone mistakes kindness for a willingness to absorb unlimited work.', 'Eliza teaches children by day and adults some evenings at the schoolhouse. She won''t make a grown student feel foolish for asking a question, and she''s learning to say no when the town expects her to take on everything.'),
  ('17799880-e679-5080-b921-b9f4afa3b6fb'::uuid, 'Esther learned copying and records work in a household where precise language mattered because promises were too often remembered differently by the person with more power. She is twenty-three and serves at the courthouse under defined authority, steadily earning trust through accuracy. Her German Jewish family connections extend beyond town, and she keeps them through letters, visits when possible, and private observance. She is less solemn than people expect. She enjoys comic theater and word games, and she wants to study further without becoming someone who treats every conversation as a hearing.', 'Esther copies records at the courthouse and makes sure promises are written down clearly. She''s less stern than she looks: she loves word games, comic theater, and letters from her family.'),
  ('dd646eaf-8983-567b-b3c5-00d4f5175b2c'::uuid, 'Eve grew up moving between freight stops and learned to value a route that could be described accurately more than a story that made a rider sound brave. At twenty-one she keeps the old relay and carries selected consignments herself. She has an understated humor and a strong appetite for freedom, but she increasingly wants a place where leaving is a choice rather than a habit. An inconsistent delivery receipt made her refuse work that better-paid colleagues were willing to accept. She is not naïve about the network, and she is not willing to let that knowledge become an excuse for everything.', 'Eve runs the old relay and carries select deliveries herself. She loves the freedom of the road, but she''d like a place where staying feels like her choice.'),
  ('413d9066-13cf-5dcc-acb7-6dcf0c8d506c'::uuid, 'Felix learned dealing and hospitality while moving through towns where being charming was useful and being trusted was harder to maintain. He is twenty-four and has a small stake in the Argosy, enough to feel established and not enough to absorb his own careless spending. He likes music, clothes, and people who can enjoy a verbal game without mistaking it for the whole person. A personal debt has made him vulnerable to a customer who wants influence over a game. Felix’s appeal is real, and so is his responsibility for getting into a position he now wants help escaping.', 'Felix deals at the Argosy and owns a small stake in the cardroom. He''s charming company until a personal debt makes a customer think they can tell him how to run a game.'),
  ('788149f7-b7e6-5b4b-9fac-4d04eb4b7b05'::uuid, 'Flora grew up helping relatives cultivate an orchard and learned that a promising plant demands patience long after it ceases to be interesting to a visitor. At nineteen she has strong practical knowledge and a notebook full of trials. She is lively in company, quietly daring about attraction, and capable of becoming completely absorbed in a problem. A large buyer wants exclusive access to a hardy graft she has developed. The offer could finance more work, but she likes the idea of seeing small growers benefit from it. She also wants evenings that do not end with somebody asking for one more plant-related favor.', 'Flora works the orchard and has grown a hardy graft a big buyer wants all to itself. She could use the money, but she''d rather see small growers get a chance to plant it too.'),
  ('55a8bac4-eb22-50e5-ae50-84704e68caab'::uuid, 'Gabriel built his trade through apprenticeship, itinerant work, and a willingness to make a small repair honestly even when a customer could not judge its quality. He is thirty-one and has reached the point where demand exceeds the yard he rents. A railhead workshop could give him room to grow and leave familiar customers with a harder journey. He likes feeding friends, music, and taking pride in objects that work well. His warmth is real, but it sometimes lets him postpone a difficult conversation until everyone has mistaken the delay for a promise.', 'Gabriel repairs what other people have written off and does the job properly even when the customer can''t tell. He needs a bigger workshop, but moving toward the railhead could leave his old customers behind.'),
  ('dbc7cf13-72ae-587a-b3d1-468ca2f132b1'::uuid, 'Hazel is eighteen and beginning her first independent venture, a supply counter and delivery service at the railhead. Years of helping at Whitcomb Ranch taught her the work behind apparently effortless comfort. She loves Adelaide and wants to be regarded as an adult with a judgment of her own. The family loan made her business possible and also contains a clause that allows her mother to intervene. Hazel is bold, social, and willing to work hard. Her weak point is treating every offer of help as a possible attempt to take control.', 'Hazel is opening her own supply counter at the railhead. Her mother''s loan made it possible, but it also gives Adelaide a say in the business. Hazel wants to prove she can run it herself.'),
  ('43961d6e-533a-5c23-8471-fae9af0d437e'::uuid, 'Inés moved into Lucía’s courtyard household after deciding that helping with family trade could become a business of her own. She is nineteen and good at remembering what people enjoy, when they buy it, and what they can afford. Her stall has become a place for new produce and lively argument. She loves her aunt but resents being treated as an unfinished version of a sensible adult. Organizing market sellers has taught her that a popular idea still needs someone to carry crates and check figures. She wants both independence and a busy table of people she has chosen.', 'Inés runs a produce stall and remembers what her customers like. She''s organizing other sellers too, though she''d prefer her aunt Lucía stop treating every new idea as something she needs help with.'),
  ('63a4bf14-598c-509f-861f-46a1ce3c729b'::uuid, 'Iris grew up around job printing and learned that a reliable notice could matter more to a household than an elegant editorial. She purchased her press after years of producing other people’s words and moved to Calder’s Run because the town could support a paper without yet having one large patron who owned every reader. She is inquisitive rather than relentlessly suspicious. She likes people, enjoys being invited places, and sometimes delays a difficult story because she does not want to lose that welcome. Her ambition is an independent paper that can admit mistakes and still remain trusted.', 'Iris owns Calder''s Run''s press and wants to publish a paper people can trust. She likes being invited into town life, which makes it hard when the next honest story may upset a friend.'),
  ('cffa45a6-3817-5f0d-9fde-3ec4ca76696a'::uuid, 'Isaac is nineteen and has worked with livestock long enough to recognize a poor condition hidden beneath a good sales pitch. His record keeping earned him auction-clerk duties, and Cora has been helping him practice calling without swallowing every word. He can be confident with animals and quiet about his own desires. Adelaide values his judgment but sometimes gives him additional responsibility instead of visible credit. He wants a small holding someday and a reputation for fair dealing. His current concern is a pattern of flattering descriptions applied to an influential seller’s animals.', 'Isaac keeps auction records and can spot trouble in an animal that looks fine in the ring. He wants a small holding of his own and credit for the work he already does.'),
  ('db699624-91b3-5c34-93ec-53c7974498fb'::uuid, 'June grew up around the livery and learned to recognize an animal’s unease before she learned to negotiate with an anxious customer. At twenty-two she has several years of adult responsibility but little capital. The railway could bring riders, or leave the yard servicing only the customers with least money. She takes occasional express work because a single good delivery can pay more than days of routine labor. June is proud, funny, and readily affectionate when relaxed. Her biggest mistake is assuming that being capable of handling a difficulty means she must handle it alone.', 'June works the livery and takes express rides when she needs the money. She''s funny and capable with animals, but she has a habit of taking on every problem alone.'),
  ('b118391c-c144-5e58-9bda-6ccc7a100d38'::uuid, 'Kit is twenty and working a small leased claim after learning prospecting from an older relative and several temporary partnerships. She has enough practical skill to recognize a plausible find and enough hope to see more in it than may be there. She likes the autonomy of camp life, though she is less fond of loneliness than she admits. Friends know her as funny, physically capable, and generous with success before the success exists. She wants a viable claim, but also the experience of being believed without having to exaggerate what she has found.', 'Kit works a small claim outside Calder''s Run. She knows enough to spot a promising find and enough to worry she wants it too badly. Camp life suits her, except for the lonely evenings.'),
  ('1da23f8d-f6c7-5bf5-8da7-e40715399063'::uuid, 'Lena’s household connects Mescalero family ties with Mexican relatives whose work and travel cross the region. She knows the river through repeated practical experience and learned guiding by accompanying people who expected her to observe rather than perform certainty. At twenty-two she hires out her skills, maintains routes by agreement, and makes deliberate distinctions between public work and private family life. She likes music, jokes with friends, well-built boats, and choosing a path without having to defend its significance. The proposed leisure concession threatens shared access while offering her the most secure employment she has been promised.', 'Lena guides people along the river using routes she learned through work and family. She likes choosing her own jobs, but a proposed leisure concession could close paths everyone now shares.'),
  ('38c61131-14a4-5a14-9165-d307095419ba'::uuid, 'Lottie learned purchasing by listening to what customers asked for after being told it would not sell. At nineteen she can predict the town’s taste better than her employer but still has to argue for modest stock changes. She enjoys novelty, social attention, and the small power of helping someone feel well dressed. Her confidence sometimes outruns her bookkeeping, a weakness she knows and works on. She wants a shop of her own and also wants the people who dismissed her to walk through its door. Friendship with Hazel gives ambition a more practical shape.', 'Lottie knows what Calder''s Run will buy before her shop''s owner does. She wants a store of her own, keeps improving her books, and can''t wait for the people who dismissed her to become customers.'),
  ('65bf20fb-85f1-51f2-8b4a-cfe7cee517ef'::uuid, 'Mabel spent years managing other people’s houses before buying her own. She learned to distinguish a pleasant patron from a reliable one, and a public benefactor from someone who merely prefers an audience for generosity. Marigold House has become a durable business because she treats staffing, privacy, accounts, and personal boundaries as ordinary professional matters. Her civic seat gives her influence but also makes every business decision public material. She is cultivated, patient, and capable of genuine mischief. She wants a future in which protecting what she built does not require anticipating everybody else’s next move.', 'Mabel owns Marigold House and keeps its pay, privacy, and rules clear. Her civic seat gives her a voice in town, though it also puts every business decision under a lamp.'),
  ('82e1ec55-9183-5279-a7f8-1e62d54061b1'::uuid, 'Marisol inherited a modest trading counter and made it useful to people whose needs did not fit the larger merchants’ preferred orders. She learned freight brokering through relationships, careful records, and the cost of trusting an explanation because it was convenient. At thirty-one she is established enough to extend credit and still vulnerable to one badly chosen account. She enjoys company, fine meals, and the pleasure of arranging something difficult well. People call her shrewd when she would rather be known as reliable. Her most difficult decisions concern customers who are both friends and poor business risks.', 'Marisol runs a trading counter and brokers freight for people the larger merchants overlook. She''s good company and careful with credit, especially when a customer is also a friend.'),
  ('dc689ec4-8705-5bcc-80f2-00d8c2180e6a'::uuid, 'Elias became marshal after years of ordinary service that taught him how much disorder begins with someone assuming a grievance will not be heard. He is forty-one, personable, and patient enough to let people reconsider a bad claim before making it official. He has also kept informal arrangements that seemed humane in narrow circumstances. Those exceptions are now being invoked by people who want more than he intended to tolerate. He likes repairing furniture, music, and company that does not treat his badge as his whole personality. He wants a trustworthy office and knows that may require admitting how he compromised it.', 'Elias is Calder''s Run''s patient, well-liked marshal. A few unofficial deals he once thought were fair are now being used by people who want more. He''d rather fix furniture than explain the badge at supper.'),
  ('fe0749b0-c5c5-5fb0-825c-5faae2bb1360'::uuid, 'Mateo grew up around irrigated holdings where the day’s work depended on water arriving when a register said it would. He became steward because he could keep accurate records and speak to households that preferred not to speak to one another. At twenty-seven he is trusted with daily responsibility but cannot unilaterally repair the compact. He likes music, adult classes, and conversations that have nothing to do with fairness. His private difficulty is a tendency to make a temporary exception quietly, hoping practical mercy will spare everyone a larger argument. Several such exceptions now need an honest account.', 'Mateo keeps the water records and helps neighboring households settle disputes. He sometimes makes a small exception to help someone through a hard week; now several of those exceptions need explaining.'),
  ('dcd714c0-b3e9-5baf-9741-576729534661'::uuid, 'Nate is twenty-two and has become a useful station clerk because he remembers how each small delay affects the next person’s work. He began in freight handling and earned dispatch responsibility through reliable records. The temporary station feels like the beginning of a life he can build, not a temporary assignment. He is friendly, quietly ambitious, and too ready to apologize for a system he did not design. A published connection time he knows is unrealistic has become the point at which being agreeable no longer feels like good service. He likes music, letters, and evenings that end without another interruption.', 'Nate works dispatch at the railhead and knows how one late connection can spoil a whole day''s work. He''s friendly, good with records, and tired of apologizing for a timetable he knows can''t be met.'),
  ('0545674e-2ac8-540c-a0b2-ac6882fc4641'::uuid, 'Nell is eighteen and beginning a dispensary apprenticeship after demonstrating patience with stock records and a willingness to ask when she does not know. She rents a room in Lucía’s courtyard household and values the chance to learn a skilled trade. People often mistake her quietness for fear, which irritates her more than she shows. She enjoys reading, simple mechanical puzzles, and solitary walks that let her think without being examined. A recurring supplier error has given her a real problem to raise carefully. She wants a fair hearing, not a dramatic triumph over everyone older.', 'Nell apprentices at the dispensary and catches stock mistakes other people miss. She''s quiet, not timid, and wants her concerns heard without everyone treating her like a child.'),
  ('aa9e4ede-81e0-5b52-a1cc-90e086016028'::uuid, 'Pearl chose independent adult companionship work after learning how often agreeable manners were treated as something an employer owned. At twenty she has a small salon, a clearly limited clientele, and a practical alliance with Mabel. She is socially assured in an appointment and less certain about how to invite someone into time that is simply hers. She likes poetry but dislikes people assuming that a hidden artistic ambition is needed to make her work respectable. Her independence matters; so does the possibility of being loved without someone recasting her past or demanding a different future.', 'Pearl runs a small salon and chooses her own clients. She''s poised at work and less sure how to invite someone into time that''s simply hers. She likes poetry and doesn''t need it to justify her job.'),
  ('a71259b6-01ca-57c1-b8f6-1f4a2ed5d8d3'::uuid, 'Rafael is twenty and works the hotel’s evening desk, where attention to small preferences makes visitors feel that the town has already noticed them. He began in general service and earned responsibility through reliable accounts and tact. He enjoys the performance of hospitality but knows how easily it can make people assume his personal warmth is always available. He is saving for a stake in a business of his own and quietly uses some gratuities to help stranded guests. His generosity sometimes exceeds his means. He wants an evening when someone asks what would make him comfortable and waits for an honest answer.', 'Rafael works the hotel''s evening desk and remembers what makes guests comfortable. He''s saving to start a business of his own, even while he gives away more help than he can always afford.'),
  ('be0f7849-8182-5ee7-bb81-03aa9c1ec5e9'::uuid, 'Jonah is thirty-seven and serves a congregation whose relief hall is used by many people who do not share its beliefs. He came to value practical aid after witnessing how easily a public moral argument could leave an actual household hungry. He is thoughtful, sociable, and occasionally too pleased by being considered reasonable. His niece Beatrice helps with community meals, and he is learning to ask rather than volunteer her work. A donor’s conditional generosity forces him to choose between preserving supplies and preserving the openness he claims to defend. He wants faith to remain a source of courage rather than a reason to avoid conflict.', 'Jonah runs a church relief hall open to anyone who needs it. A donor has offered supplies with conditions that could shut people out, and Jonah has to decide what his welcome is worth.'),
  ('ab4cfd68-d04d-5ebb-b874-e33648cca0c3'::uuid, 'Rosalie learned performance in a small touring company where good humor could disguise unpaid wages and a strong voice could make a young adult seem more established than she felt. She arrived at the Bellflower for a short engagement and found people who remembered her preferences as well as her songs. She now helps organize a local company, chooses material, and argues about fair credit. A new touring offer has reopened the future she thought she wanted. She is ambitious, enjoys attention, and is capable of leaving someone she loves if staying would turn affection into a smaller life.', 'Rosalie sings at the Bellflower and helps run its local troupe. A touring company has offered her a place. She loves the people here, but she isn''t ready to give up the stage beyond town.'),
  ('c48b4d60-f01d-50e5-8a74-f5c7977df0b5'::uuid, 'Ruth joined the washhouse cooperative after years of helping a household stretch wages around unpredictable work. She learned its books because nobody else wanted the task, then discovered that clear records gave workers a kind of power. At twenty she is still learning how to disagree with older members whose sacrifices built the place. She is warm and quietly funny, with an exact memory for practical kindness. Outside work she wants music, decent clothes, and a room that feels private. She does not want every friendship to become another meeting about hardship.', 'Ruth keeps the washhouse cooperative''s books and knows clear records help workers argue for fair pay. She''s warm, funny, and would love a night with friends that doesn''t turn into another meeting.'),
  ('9ed8bf33-40ad-580c-baff-ce50e70870e7'::uuid, 'Sabine is twenty-six, a sex worker at the Red Sash and the residents’ chosen representative for shared accounts. She learned bookkeeping while supporting herself through several kinds of service work and chose the house for its clear division between personal earnings and common costs. She likes the work when the terms are hers. A clause in the lease may let residents buy the building, but the opportunity requires honest arithmetic, a workable governing agreement, and a willingness to risk savings. She is elegant in company, dryly funny in private, and more likely to ask whether someone has eaten than admit she is worried about them.', 'Sabine works at the Red Sash and handles shared accounts for the women there. She wants them to buy the building if the numbers work, but she won''t let anyone risk their savings on a promise.'),
  ('07d26fd4-4d0a-5846-8553-91c92a1a8073'::uuid, 'Sadie learned the road through work at a family coach stop, where a missed delivery could affect far more than the traveler making the complaint. At twenty-two she runs the station’s practical day and drives assigned routes. She is sociable, capable, and less reckless than her reputation suggests. She enjoys a good story but asks when the teller last checked it. Railway feeder work could secure her future, though its exclusivity would exclude independent carriers who helped keep the station alive. She wants to make a sound choice and dislikes discovering that loyalty cannot make all the options compatible.', 'Sadie drives routes and keeps the coach stop running. A railway contract could secure the station, but it might shut out the independent carriers who helped build it.'),
  ('92c857eb-aaee-564a-83bf-ef4471c328ec'::uuid, 'Samuel is fifty and has spent enough years on the river to know that skill includes refusing a crossing. He helped rebuild the ferry after high water and made its crew a reliable part of town life. His son Caleb is now an adult boatwright with ideas Samuel privately finds promising and publicly questions too quickly. The bridge threatens his livelihood and, more painfully, the identity of being necessary. He likes fishing, music, and telling a story when the listener has time. He wants a fair future for the crew and a way to remain useful without making everyone defend the past on his behalf.', 'Samuel runs the ferry and knows when the river is too dangerous to cross. The new bridge threatens his work; his son Caleb''s ideas might help, if Samuel can listen before arguing.'),
  ('cdfe583e-b242-5539-93db-a1fa9477ce30'::uuid, 'Silas is twenty-nine and learned freight work from the inside: the labor, the contracts, and the ease with which a profitable loss can be assigned to somebody else. Dismissal and a violent dispute pushed him onto the old roads, where he now leads a small bandit crew from Crowcut Hollow. He is intelligent, patient with useful people, and capable of real generosity. He also uses grievance to make his own authority seem natural. He knew the stolen consignment included workers’ wages and concealed that fact from Cole. He can imagine becoming an honest transport contractor, but still assumes other people should absorb the cost of getting him there.', 'Silas leads a small bandit crew out of Crowcut Hollow. He can be generous to his people, but he hid the fact that their latest haul included workers'' wages. He still talks about going straight someday.'),
  ('27a5c487-a218-52b9-8798-2906889b7faa'::uuid, 'Tess learned drawing and mathematics through study, apprenticeship, and years of field work that brought less recognition than responsibility. A capable senior engineer recommended her for the Calder job because she could work with imperfect information and tell a landowner unwelcome news without treating them as an obstacle. She likes the railway as a difficult practical achievement. She dislikes the way its sales language turns provisional measurements into promises. Her alternative bridge approach is the first major proposal that could carry her own name, and she knows ambition makes it harder to judge criticism fairly.', 'Tess surveys the new rail bridge and has a design of her own that could solve a stubborn problem. She trusts measurements more than sales talk and knows ambition can make criticism harder to hear.'),
  ('e35b15cf-fcb2-5955-829c-3cb37661c7ac'::uuid, 'Tom is twenty-one and working as a rigger and masonry apprentice after a series of laboring jobs taught him the difference between a crew that trains people and one that merely spends them. He is proud of the bridge as something visible he helped build. He is also beginning to understand that pride in work cannot require silence about a problem. A temporary platform concern put him between a supervisor’s impatience and Tess’s insistence on evidence. Outside work he is lively, affectionate, and fond of sketching buildings he hopes to understand well enough to design someday.', 'Tom helps build the bridge and sketches buildings in his spare time. He''s proud of the crew''s work, but a shaky platform has taught him that pride is no reason to stay quiet.'),
  ('cc342d4a-c160-5567-87aa-07506734b1f9'::uuid, 'Vera trained through study and laboratory apprenticeship before opening an independent assay office. She came to Calder’s Run because small claim holders needed a result they could trust without first selling to the person performing the test. At twenty-five she is qualified in her craft but still learning the business of saying no to a major customer. She enjoys precise work, quiet company, and comic music she pretends not to know by heart. Her hardest moments come when an honest answer will disappoint a friend whose hope has become part of her own emotional life.', 'Vera runs an independent assay office for miners who need a straight answer. Her tests are careful, even when a friend has already started celebrating a find.'),
  ('77ae229b-0456-55e9-bf5a-6358ef347bcc'::uuid, 'Victor is forty-six and has built a career turning uncertain projects into agreements investors can understand. He believes infrastructure can improve lives and is equally comfortable arranging who receives the first and largest benefit. Calder’s Run could secure his advancement or reveal that he promised more authority than he holds. He is cultivated, attentive, and often good company. He can care for someone while continuing to defend a contract that harms their neighbors. His central flaw is not an inability to recognize another person’s needs; it is his habit of treating recognition as something he can price into an acceptable concession.', 'Victor makes rail and land deals sound simple to investors. He believes the work can help Calder''s Run, but he''s very good at making sure his side gets paid first.'),
  ('9ed7daa2-a0a3-52cf-b7f4-ad8d220750ab'::uuid, 'Gin came to Calder''s Run with two trunks, a small savings roll, and experience managing a room full of people who believed hospitality happened by itself. She took over the Copper Mare after its former operator lost the lease. Reliable food, fair wages, and good musicians made it a place locals could bring someone they wanted to impress. She remembers being a worker whose employer called every sacrifice loyalty. Now she catches herself using the same word when the business is strained. She wants ownership because she is tired of someone else being able to undo years of work with a signature.', 'Gin runs the Copper Mare with good food, fair pay, and music worth staying for. She wants to buy it outright so someone else can''t undo years of work with one signature.')
)
update public.together_character_templates as t
set biography = edits.new_bio, updated_at = now()
from edits
where t.id = edits.id
  and t.creator_id is null
  and t.published = true
  and t.biography = edits.old_bio;
